A||N||4||jhogans||Engine Conversion HP Info||||1||
Z||000000||jhogans||06-16-2000||05:30 PM||||I was looking through my old emails and I found a email from Ed Parks from the Fiero Factory.<P>******************************<BR>Absolute ease is turbo on your 2.8 (173 ci). Should yield about 200-210 hp. Cost approx $3200. owner installed.<P>Next easiest: 3.4, 207 ci (Camaro/Firebird) with headers. Should yield 185-195 hp, Cost approx $3000 installed. Without headers, about 175 hp, and $2500. <P>Next is turbo on 3.4 (207 ci). Should yield 240-250 hp. Cost is approx $5700, with owner installing the turbo.<P>3.4 TDC produces 215 hp. 3800 (231 ci) with supercharger is 240 hp.<P>Northstar is either 275 or 300 hp (286 ci.). We no longer do these conversions, but he can expect $8-13K.<P>Newer technology can get over 1 hp per ci, with reliability. But that will cost for the conversion. Older technology can get 1 hp per ci, but reliabilty (and cost) is better.<P>Ed Parks, from The Fiero Factory<BR>8710B Hwy 53, Toney, Ala 35773<BR>Shop: 256-420-5391...Hm: 256-882-1568 <A HREF="http://www.welcome.to/thefierofactory" TARGET=_blank>http://www.welcome.to/thefierofactory</A> <BR>******************************<P><BR>I have a question pertaining to the 3.4 w/Turbo install.  Is it possible to pop in a twin turbo for the 3.4?  If so, how much HP can I expect.<P>I was considering a Northstar Install or SBC install but I am now considering the 3.4 w/turbo setup.  Which will be done by Ed Parks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT Bastard||06-16-2000||08:37 PM||||Good luck cramming it in there.  If you're having someone else do the work, you'd better prepare to bend-over.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||olympic||06-16-2000||09:10 PM||||  I seen a single turbo 3.4 TDC race motor on the net once.  They claimed 350 hp.  With a twin turbo, lowered compression and some serious boost, 400-500 hp would not be out of the question.  The TDC is a strong engine(forged crank, rods, pistons) and can flow ALOT of air through its heads.  So if your determined and got the cash I say go for it.  Just remember that everything you need will have to be custom made which is either very expensive or time consuming.  Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||06-17-2000||01:09 AM||||How long do you want it to last?||||reg||1
Z||000004||MikeE||06-17-2000||08:03 AM||||I saw the HP figures that olympic posted for the turbo 3.4 TDC but I assume you're using the standard pushrod 3.4.<P>A twin turbo does not necessarily make more HP than a single turbo.  The HP depends on the size of the turbo and the amount of boost you dial in at the wastegate. The 240-250 HP Ed Parks quoted is for the particular combo he's using.  How much boost you can handle depends on how well you can control detonation and how strong the engine is or, as TerryK put it, how long you want it to last.  Look at how much HP the Buick GN guys are getting with a single turbo.  The main advantage of a twin turbo, especially at higher boost levels, is that you can acheive a particular level of boost with smaller turbos and they spool up faster, producing less of the dreaded "turbo lag".||||reg||1
